---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Logo from '../components/Logo.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import {
	CONTENT_LEXICON,
	CONTENT_LEXICON_ID,
	CONTENT_LEXICON_DESCRIPTION,
	contentSchemaFields,
} from '../lib/lexicon/schema-doc';
import { parseInlineCode } from '../lib/lexicon/inline-code';
import { highlightSource } from '../lib/reader/highlight';

const contentFields = contentSchemaFields();
const schemaJson = JSON.stringify( CONTENT_LEXICON, null, 2 );
// The schema is JSON we control, so highlight it directly (known language) rather
// than auto-detecting. Server-side at build time → no client JS, like the reader.
const schemaJsonHtml = highlightSource( schemaJson, 'json' );

// A faithful, trimmed slice of a real published article's content object (the
// "Potato buns, one way" recipe), so the page can show how the block tree
// actually nests rather than describe it abstractly. The live record has 17
// top-level blocks; this keeps the first heading and a two-item list, with the
// `$type` union discriminator and `version` intact.
const exampleContent = {
	$type: 'blog.skypress.content.gutenberg',
	version: 1,
	blocks: [
		{
			name: 'core/heading',
			attributes: { level: 2, content: 'Ingredients' },
			innerBlocks: [],
		},
		{
			name: 'core/list',
			attributes: { ordered: false, values: '' },
			innerBlocks: [
				{
					name: 'core/list-item',
					attributes: { content: '600g T45 flour' },
					innerBlocks: [],
				},
				{
					name: 'core/list-item',
					attributes: { content: '200g warm milk' },
					innerBlocks: [],
				},
			],
		},
	],
};
const exampleJson = JSON.stringify( exampleContent, null, 2 );
const exampleJsonHtml = highlightSource( exampleJson, 'json' );
---

<Base
	title="The lexicon: how SkyPress stores your writing"
	description="The open AT Protocol records SkyPress writes to your account when you publish: a publication, a document with your block content, and a Bluesky post linking back."
>
	<header class="lex-masthead">
		<a class="lex-home" href="/" aria-label="SkyPress home"><Logo /></a>
		<a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/write">Start writing</a>
	</header>

	<main class="lex">
		<p class="lex-eyebrow">The open format</p>
		<h1 class="lex-title">Your words live in <em>your</em> account.</h1>
		<p class="lex-lede">
			When you publish with SkyPress, nothing gets locked inside SkyPress. Your
			writing is saved to your own AT&nbsp;Protocol account, in open formats any
			reader on the social web can understand. You can take it anywhere. Here&rsquo;s
			exactly what we write, and why.
		</p>

		<section class="lex-section">
			<h2>What gets written when you publish</h2>
			<p>Publishing creates a few records on your PDS (the data store behind your account):</p>
			<ul class="lex-records">
				<li>
					<code>site.standard.publication</code>: your SkyPress home. We create
					it once, then reuse it.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>site.standard.document</code>: the article itself, with its
					title, a plain-text copy, and your block content.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>app.bsky.feed.post</code>: a public Bluesky post that links back
					to the article, so people can find it.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The first two use the community
				<a href="https://tangled.org/standard.site/lexicons" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">standard.site</a>
				lexicons, not anything SkyPress-specific, so other tools and readers that
				already speak them can pick up your writing too. The one format SkyPress
				actually owns is the block content tucked inside the document; that&rsquo;s
				the part below.
			</p>
			<p class="lex-honest">
				One thing worth repeating: publishing also creates a public Bluesky post.
				We&rsquo;ll always remind you before you hit publish&nbsp;:)
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="lex-section">
			<h2>The format we own: <code>{ CONTENT_LEXICON_ID }</code></h2>
			<p>{ parseInlineCode( CONTENT_LEXICON_DESCRIPTION ).map( ( s ) => ( s.code ? <code>{ s.text }</code> : s.text ) ) }</p>
			<table class="lex-table">
				<thead>
					<tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Required</th><th scope="col">What it is</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					{ contentFields.map( ( f ) => (
						<tr>
							<td><code>{ f.name }</code></td>
							<td>{ f.type }</td>
							<td>{ f.required ? 'yes' : 'optional' }</td>
							<td>{ parseInlineCode( f.description ).map( ( s ) => ( s.code ? <code>{ s.text }</code> : s.text ) ) }</td>
						</tr>
					) ) }
				</tbody>
			</table>
			<p class="lex-note">
				This is the only lexicon SkyPress owns. If a reader doesn&rsquo;t understand the
				format, it falls back to the document&rsquo;s plain-text
				<code>textContent</code>, so your article is never unreadable. Here&rsquo;s
				the full schema:
			</p>
			<pre class="lex-code"><code class="hljs language-json" set:html={ schemaJsonHtml } /></pre>

			<h3>What the blocks look like</h3>
			<p>
				The schema says <code>blocks</code> is &ldquo;the block tree exactly as
				produced by the editor&rdquo;, which is a bit abstract. So here&rsquo;s a
				real one. This is the <code>content</code> object from a published recipe,
				<a href="/@jeherve.com/recipes/3mnwlydxcr22u">Potato buns, one way</a>,
				trimmed to its first heading and a two-item list:
			</p>
			<pre class="lex-code"><code class="hljs language-json" set:html={ exampleJsonHtml } /></pre>
			<p>
				Every block is the same little shape: a <code>name</code> (the Gutenberg
				block, like <code>core/heading</code>), an <code>attributes</code> object
				for its content and settings, and <code>innerBlocks</code> for anything
				nested inside it, which is how the list here holds its items. The same
				shape repeats all the way down. The <code>$type</code> tells any reader
				which format it&rsquo;s looking at; everything else is just blocks. You can
				see the full record behind any article yourself with the
				&ldquo;view&nbsp;record&rdquo; link in its eyebrow&nbsp;:)
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="lex-section">
			<h2>Want to run your own?</h2>
			<p>
				SkyPress is open source (GPL-2.0-or-later), and it&rsquo;s only ever an
				editor, an OAuth client, and a public renderer, never a home for your
				data. Your writing already lives in your AT&nbsp;Protocol account, in the
				open records above, so nothing&rsquo;s tied to this particular site.
			</p>
			<p>
				If you&rsquo;d rather not use skypress.blog, you can host your own copy. It&rsquo;s
				an Astro app that deploys to Cloudflare; the only thing you need to set is a
				<code>PUBLIC_SITE_URL</code>, and the OAuth client metadata and redirect URIs
				adapt to wherever you put it. The code, and the setup steps, live in the repo:
				<a href="https://tangled.org/jeremy.herve.bzh/skypress" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">tangled.org/jeremy.herve.bzh/skypress</a>.
				Fork it, send patches, or just have a read&nbsp;:)
			</p>
		</section>

		<div class="lex-actions">
			<a class="btn btn--primary" href="/write">Start writing</a>
		</div>
	</main>

	<Footer />
</Base>

<style>
	.lex-masthead {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: space-between;
		padding: 1.5rem clamp( 1.25rem, 5vw, 4rem );
		border-bottom: 1px solid var( --line );
	}
	.lex-home { text-decoration: none; }

	.lex {
		max-width: 48rem;
		margin: 0 auto;
		padding: clamp( 2.5rem, 7vh, 4.5rem ) clamp( 1.25rem, 5vw, 4rem ) 4rem;
	}
	.lex-eyebrow {
		font-family: var( --font-mono );
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.12em;
		font-size: 0.72rem;
		color: var( --sun );
		margin: 0;
	}
	.lex-title {
		font-size: clamp( 2.2rem, 6vw, 3.4rem );
		font-weight: 760;
		letter-spacing: -0.03em;
		line-height: 1.02;
		margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
	}
	.lex-title em { font-style: italic; color: var( --sun ); }
	.lex-lede {
		font-size: clamp( 1.1rem, 2.2vw, 1.3rem );
		line-height: 1.6;
		color: var( --ink-soft );
		margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
	}

	.lex-section {
		margin-top: 3rem;
		padding-top: 2.5rem;
		border-top: 1px solid var( --line );
	}
	.lex-section h2 {
		font-size: 1.5rem;
		letter-spacing: -0.02em;
		margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
	}
	.lex-section h3 {
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		margin: 2rem 0 0.6rem;
	}
	.lex-section p { color: var( --ink-soft ); line-height: 1.65; }
	.lex-section code {
		font-family: var( --font-mono );
		font-size: 0.86em;
		background: var( --sun-tint );
		color: var( --sun-strong );
		padding: 0.08em 0.36em;
		border-radius: 5px;
	}
	/* Long NSIDs (e.g. blog.skypress.content.gutenberg) have no spaces, so let
	   them break inside headings rather than overflow narrow viewports. */
	.lex-section h2 code,
	.lex-section h3 code {
		overflow-wrap: anywhere;
		word-break: break-word;
	}

	.lex-records { padding-left: 1.2rem; color: var( --ink-soft ); line-height: 1.7; }
	.lex-records li { margin: 0.35rem 0; }
	.lex-honest { color: var( --muted ); font-size: 0.95rem; }
	.lex-note { margin-top: 1.25rem; }

	.lex-table {
		width: 100%;
		border-collapse: collapse;
		margin: 1rem 0 0;
		font-size: 0.95rem;
	}
	.lex-table th,
	.lex-table td {
		text-align: left;
		vertical-align: top;
		padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var( --line );
	}
	.lex-table th {
		font-family: var( --font-mono );
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.04em;
		font-size: 0.7rem;
		color: var( --muted );
	}
	.lex-table td { color: var( --ink-soft ); }
	.lex-table td:first-child code { white-space: nowrap; }

	.lex-code {
		margin-top: 1.25rem;
		background: var( --paper-raised );
		border: 1px solid var( --line );
		border-radius: 10px;
		padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
		overflow: auto;
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		line-height: 1.55;
		/* String/number hues that read on the light code surface; the warm-orange
		   and muted token colours below reuse brand tokens that already flip. */
		--code-string: #3f7d4f;
		--code-number: #2f6f86;
	}
	.lex-code code {
		font-family: var( --font-mono );
		background: none;
		color: var( --ink );
		padding: 0;
	}
	/* highlight.js token colours, scoped to this light code block (unlike the
	   reader's dark <pre>). The spans arrive via set:html, so they need :global.
	   No background/padding on .hljs, so the .lex-code chrome above is untouched. */
	.lex-code :global( .hljs-comment ),
	.lex-code :global( .hljs-quote ),
	.lex-code :global( .hljs-punctuation ) {
		color: var( --muted );
	}
	.lex-code :global( .hljs-comment ),
	.lex-code :global( .hljs-quote ) {
		font-style: italic;
	}
	.lex-code :global( .hljs-attr ),
	.lex-code :global( .hljs-keyword ),
	.lex-code :global( .hljs-selector-tag ),
	.lex-code :global( .hljs-name ) {
		color: var( --sun-strong );
	}
	.lex-code :global( .hljs-string ),
	.lex-code :global( .hljs-symbol ),
	.lex-code :global( .hljs-meta .hljs-string ) {
		color: var( --code-string );
	}
	.lex-code :global( .hljs-number ),
	.lex-code :global( .hljs-literal ),
	.lex-code :global( .hljs-built_in ) {
		color: var( --code-number );
	}

	@media ( prefers-color-scheme: dark ) {
		.lex-code {
			--code-string: #8fc98f;
			--code-number: #7fb2c9;
		}
	}

	.lex-actions {
		display: flex;
		gap: 0.85rem;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		margin-top: 3rem;
		padding-top: 2.5rem;
		border-top: 1px solid var( --line );
	}
</style>
